                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "6", item: "eggs, beaten"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 tsp", item: "espelette pepper (or sweet paprika plus a pinch of cayenne)"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "3 tbsp", item: "olive oil"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 tsp", item: "salt"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1", item: "crusty baguette, for the table"),
               ],
               steps: [
                   "Warm the olive oil in a wide skillet over medium heat and cook the onion and peppers with a pinch of salt until completely limp and sweet, a full 15 minutes. No browning, no shortcuts.",
                   "Add the garlic and cook 2 minutes more, until fragrant, then stir in the tomatoes, espelette, and remaining salt.",
                   "Simmer uncovered, stirring now and then, until the tomatoes collapse and the mixture thickens from watery to spoonably soft, 12 to 15 minutes.",
                   "Taste the stew before the eggs go in. It should already be delicious on its own, sweet and gently warm; fix the salt now, because you cannot stir hard later.",
                   "Lower the heat, pour the beaten eggs over the vegetables, and fold slowly with a spatula just until the eggs form soft, creamy curds threaded through the peppers, about 2 minutes.",
                   "Pull the pan off the heat while the eggs still look slightly underdone; they finish in the pan. Serve straight from the skillet with torn baguette.",
               ],
               note: "The eggs are a binder, not an omelet. Fold them in barely and stop early; one minute too long turns silk into cafeteria scramble.",
               shortcut: "A drained 14-oz can of diced tomatoes replaces fresh out of season. The stew is a shade less bright, so add a pinch of sugar."),
        Recipe(dishID: "france-quenelles", serves: 4, prepMinutes: 45, cookMinutes: 50, difficulty: 3,
               ingredients: [
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 lb", item: "skinless pike or cod fillets, very cold"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "3/4 cup", item: "whole milk"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "5 tbsp", item: "butter"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "3/4 cup", item: "all-purpose flour"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "3", item: "eggs"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 1/4 cups", item: "heavy cream"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1/2 lb", item: "shell-on shrimp"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 tbsp", item: "tomato paste"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1/4 tsp", item: "ground nutmeg"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 1/2 tsp", item: "salt"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1/4 tsp", item: "white pepper"),
               ],
               steps: [
                   "Make the panade: boil the milk with 3 tbsp of the butter, dump in the flour, and stir hard over the heat until it forms a ball that films the pan. Cool completely.",
                   "Peel the shrimp, saving every shell. Blitz the cold fish in a food processor to a fine paste, then add the cooled panade, eggs, nutmeg, salt, and white pepper and blend until smooth.",
                   "With the machine running, stream in 1/2 cup of the cream. Chill the mixture at least 30 minutes; a cold paste holds its shape, a warm one dissolves in the poaching water.",
                   "Make the sauce, the Nantua stand-in: sizzle the shrimp shells in the remaining butter until pink and toasty, add the tomato paste and remaining cream, simmer 10 minutes, and strain, pressing hard on the shells.",
                   "Shape the paste into fat ovals using two large spoons dipped in hot water, passing each scoop between the spoons until smooth.",
                   "Poach the quenelles in barely simmering salted water, never boiling, until they roll over and feel set, 8 to 10 minutes. Drain on a towel.",
                   "Lay the quenelles in a buttered baking dish, pour the sauce over, and add the peeled shrimp. Bake at 400°F for 15 minutes, until puffed and blistering at the edges.",
                   "Serve straight from the oven, before they deflate, with rice or bread for the sauce. The collapse starts within minutes; in Lyon they run these to the table.",
               ],
               note: "Cold ingredients and a lazy poach are the two hinges. If the water boils the quenelles shred, and if the paste is warm they never hold at all.",
               shortcut: "Skip the shell sauce and stir the tomato paste into cream with a splash of bottled clam juice. Five minutes instead of twenty; shallower, still pink and plush."),
        Recipe(dishID: "france-tartiflette", serves: 4, prepMinutes: 20, cookMinutes: 50, difficulty: 1,
               ingredients: [
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "2 lb", item: "yukon gold potatoes"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 lb", item: "reblochon-style washed-rind cheese (or ripe camembert)"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "6 oz", item: "thick-cut bacon, cut in batons"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 large", item: "yellow onion, sliced"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1/3 cup", item: "dry white wine"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1/2 cup", item: "crème fraîche or heavy cream"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 clove", item: "garlic, halved"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 tsp", item: "salt"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1/2 tsp", item: "black pepper"),
               ],
               steps: [
                   "Boil the potatoes whole in well-salted water until just tender, about 20 minutes. Drain, cool enough to handle, and slice into thick coins; leave the skins on.",
                   "Cook the bacon in a skillet until its fat runs and the edges crisp, then add the onion and cook until soft and golden, about 10 minutes.",
                   "Splash in the wine and let it bubble away almost completely, scraping the pan. This is the traditional deglaze and it cooks off entirely.",
                   "Rub a baking dish all over with the cut garlic clove, then layer in the potatoes, bacon-onion mixture, salt, pepper, and dollops of crème fraîche, folding gently.",
                   "Halve the cheese horizontally through its equator and lay both halves on top, rind side up, like two shields covering the dish.",
                   "Bake at 400°F for 25 to 30 minutes, until the rind blisters brown and molten cheese runs into every gap. Rest 5 minutes, then scoop at the table with green salad alongside.",
               ],
               note: "Rind side up is the trick; the rind roasts into a savory lid while the underside melts downward. True raw-milk reblochon is unavailable in the US, so buy the closest washed-rind wheel.",
               shortcut: "Use leftover boiled or roasted potatoes and the dish comes together in 10 minutes of assembly. Slightly drier, easily fixed with an extra spoon of cream."),
        Recipe(dishID: "france-canele", serves: 6, prepMinutes: 25, cookMinutes: 65, difficulty: 3,
               ingredients: [
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "2 cups", item: "whole milk"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "3 tbsp", item: "butter, plus more for the molds"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1", item: "vanilla bean, split (or 1 tbsp vanilla extract)"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "2", item: "egg yolks"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1", item: "whole egg"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 cup", item: "sugar"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "3/4 cup", item: "all-purpose flour"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1/4 tsp", item: "salt"),
               ],
               steps: [
                   "Heat the milk with the butter and vanilla bean until steaming, then let it cool until just warm. Hot milk would scramble the eggs and toughen the batter.",
                   "Whisk the yolks, whole egg, and sugar together, then whisk in the flour and salt, and finally the warm milk in a slow stream. The batter should be thin, like crepe batter.",
                   "Strain the batter, cover, and refrigerate at least 24 hours and up to 48. This rest is not optional; it relaxes the flour so the centers set to custard instead of cake.",
                   "Heat the oven to 475°F. Butter canele molds or a dark metal muffin tin generously and put the empty tin in the oven for 5 minutes until the butter sizzles.",
                   "Stir the cold batter gently, without whipping in air, and pour it into the hot molds, stopping 1/2 inch from the rim.",
                   "Bake 15 minutes at 475°F, then drop the oven to 375°F and bake 40 to 50 minutes more. Hold your nerve: they are done when nearly black-brown, not merely golden.",
                   "Turn them out of the molds onto a rack within a minute of leaving the oven and cool at least 30 minutes. The shell crisps as it cools; eat them the same day.",
               ],
               note: "Every failed canele is an underbaked canele. The mahogany-going-on-black crust looks burnt to first-timers and is exactly the bitter caramel shell the custard needs.",
               shortcut: "A dark nonstick muffin tin replaces copper molds and beeswax. You lose the deep flutes and some crust snap, but the custard interior is untouched."),
    ]

    private static let recipes_spain: [Recipe] = [
        Recipe(dishID: "spain-paella", serves: 4, prepMinutes: 20, cookMinutes: 60, difficulty: 2,
               ingredients: [
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 1/2 cups", item: "bomba or calasparra rice (or another short-grain rice, not risotto rice)"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 1/2 lb", item: "bone-in chicken thighs, cut in half"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "3/4 lb", item: "rabbit pieces (or more chicken thighs)"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1/2 lb", item: "flat romano or green beans, cut in 2-inch lengths"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 cup", item: "frozen butter beans or lima beans"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1", item: "ripe tomato, grated on a box grater"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 big pinch", item: "saffron threads"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 tsp", item: "sweet smoked paprika"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1/4 cup", item: "olive oil"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "5 cups", item: "chicken stock, hot"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 sprig", item: "fresh rosemary"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "2 tsp", item: "salt"),
               ],
               steps: [
                   "Heat the oil in your widest shallow pan, 13 inches or more, over medium-high. Salt the chicken and rabbit and brown them slowly and thoroughly, a full 15 minutes; this browning is the flavor base.",
                   "Push the meat to the edges, add the green beans and butter beans to the center, and cook 3 minutes. Then add the grated tomato and paprika and fry until it darkens to brick and smells sweet.",
                   "Pour in the hot stock, crumble in the saffron, and season the liquid until it tastes slightly too salty on its own; the rice will absorb the excess.",
                   "Simmer the broth with the meat for 10 minutes so it takes on the pan's flavor, then scatter in the rice as evenly as you can. From this moment on, never stir again.",
                   "Cook hard for 10 minutes, then lower the heat and cook 8 more, rotating the pan for even heat. Lay the rosemary sprig on top in the final minutes.",
                   "Listen at the end: a faint crackle means the socarrat, the prized toasted crust, is forming underneath. Raise the heat 60 to 90 seconds, sniffing for toast, not burn.",
