               shortcut: "Skip the sieve if your blender is powerful and you like rustic texture. You save 5 minutes and lose the silky restaurant finish."),
        Recipe(dishID: "spain-pulpo", serves: 4, prepMinutes: 10, cookMinutes: 70, difficulty: 2,
               ingredients: [
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "2-3 lb", item: "frozen octopus, thawed in the refrigerator"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1", item: "yellow onion, halved"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "2", item: "bay leaves"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 1/2 lb", item: "yellow potatoes"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "2 tsp", item: "sweet smoked paprika"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "2 tsp", item: "coarse sea salt"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1/3 cup", item: "olive oil, the best you have"),
               ],
               steps: [
                   "Bring a large pot of unsalted water to a rolling boil with the onion and bay leaves. Freezing has already tenderized the octopus, which is why frozen beats fresh here.",
                   "Perform the traditional dip: holding the octopus by the head with tongs, lower it into the boiling water for 3 seconds and lift it out. Repeat three times; this sets the skin so it does not slough off.",
                   "Lower the octopus in for good and simmer gently, uncovered, for 40 to 50 minutes. It is done when a knife slides easily into the thickest part of a tentacle.",
                   "Turn off the heat and let the octopus rest in its cooking water for 15 minutes; this rest finishes the tenderizing that no amount of extra boiling can.",
                   "Meanwhile, boil the potatoes in some of the octopus water until tender, then slice into thick coins and lay them on a wooden board or warm platter.",
                   "Snip the tentacles into 1/2-inch coins with kitchen scissors, right over the potatoes. Shower everything with the coarse salt and paprika, flood with olive oil, and serve with toothpicks.",
               ],
               note: "Buy frozen without apology; Galician pulpeiras freeze theirs too. The knife-tender check and the 15-minute rest in the pot matter more than any clock time.",
               shortcut: "Spanish groceries and some supermarkets sell cooked octopus tentacles vacuum-packed. Warm them 2 minutes in simmering water and jump to the final step."),
        Recipe(dishID: "spain-tortilla", serves: 4, prepMinutes: 15, cookMinutes: 35, difficulty: 2,
               ingredients: [
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 1/2 lb", item: "yukon gold potatoes, peeled and sliced 1/8 inch thick"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "6", item: "eggs"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1", item: "yellow onion, thinly sliced"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 1/2 cups", item: "olive oil"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 1/4 tsp", item: "salt"),
               ],
               steps: [
                   "Warm the olive oil in a 9- or 10-inch nonstick skillet over medium-low and slide in the potatoes and onion. They should poach lazily in the oil, never fry or brown.",
                   "Cook 20 to 25 minutes, turning gently now and then, until a potato slice breaks in half under a spoon with no resistance. Drain everything in a colander set over a bowl, saving the oil.",
                   "Beat the eggs with the salt in a large bowl, fold in the warm drained potatoes, and let the mixture sit for 10 minutes; this soak is where potato and egg become one thing.",
                   "Heat 2 tbsp of the saved oil in the same skillet over medium-high until shimmering, pour in the mixture, and immediately drop the heat to medium-low, shaking the pan so nothing grabs.",
                   "Cook 4 to 5 minutes, tucking the edges under with a spatula to build the tortilla's rounded shoulder, until the bottom is set but the top is still loose.",
                   "The flip: put a flat plate over the skillet, grip both with conviction, and invert in one motion over the sink. Slide the tortilla back in, raw side down, and cook 2 to 3 minutes more.",
                   "Turn it onto a plate while the center is still barely set; it firms as it rests. Serve warm or at room temperature, in wedges, never refrigerated the first day.",
               ],
               note: "A jiggly center is correct in most of Spain; carryover heat finishes it on the plate. The only real failure is overcooked, bouncy egg.",
               shortcut: "Poach the potatoes in the microwave with 3 tbsp oil, covered, 8 minutes. Saves a cup of oil and half the time; you lose the deep olive oil savor."),
        Recipe(dishID: "spain-fabada", serves: 6, prepMinutes: 15, cookMinutes: 180, difficulty: 2,
               ingredients: [
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 lb", item: "dried large white beans (fabes, or large lima or butter beans)"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1/2 lb", item: "spanish chorizo (the cured kind, not mexican)"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1/2 lb", item: "morcilla (or double the chorizo)"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "3/4 lb", item: "slab bacon or salt pork, in one piece"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1", item: "yellow onion, halved"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "3 cloves", item: "garlic, peeled"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 1/2 tsp", item: "sweet smoked paprika"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 pinch", item: "saffron threads"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "2 tbsp", item: "olive oil"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "2 tsp", item: "salt"),
               ],
               steps: [
                   "The night before, cover the beans generously with cold water and leave them on the counter. They should double in size; skip this and no amount of simmering saves them.",
                   "Drain the beans into a wide pot with the onion, garlic, olive oil, and the bacon in one piece. Cover with fresh cold water by 1 inch and bring just to a boil.",
                   "Skim the foam, then 'scare' the beans the Asturian way: pour in a splash of cold water to knock the boil back. Do this twice more during cooking; it keeps the skins from splitting.",
                   "Lower the heat until the surface barely shivers, add the paprika and saffron, and cook uncovered 2 hours, shaking the pot occasionally instead of stirring so the beans stay whole.",
                   "Add the chorizo and morcilla, tucking them under the surface, and cook 45 minutes to 1 hour more, until the beans are butter-soft and the broth has thickened on its own.",
                   "Season with the salt only near the end, then rest off the heat for 20 minutes. Slice the meats, return them to the pot, and serve in deep bowls with bread and no plans afterward.",
               ],
               note: "Never stir, only swirl. The beans must arrive at the bowl whole and creamy, and one enthusiastic spoon can turn three hours of patience into bean puree.",
               shortcut: "Two 15-oz cans of butter beans, added in the final 45 minutes with all the meats, cut the time to one hour. The broth stays thinner."),
        Recipe(dishID: "spain-calcots", serves: 4, prepMinutes: 25, cookMinutes: 25, difficulty: 2,
               ingredients: [
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "3 bunches", item: "fat scallions or spring onions, the thickest you can find"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1/2 cup", item: "roasted almonds"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1/4 cup", item: "roasted hazelnuts"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 jar (12 oz)", item: "roasted red peppers, drained"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1", item: "ripe tomato"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "2 cloves", item: "garlic"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 slice", item: "stale bread, toasted"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "2 tbsp", item: "sherry vinegar"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1/2 cup", item: "olive oil"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 tsp", item: "sweet smoked paprika"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 tsp", item: "salt"),
               ],
               steps: [
                   "Make the romesco first: blitz the almonds, hazelnuts, and toasted bread in a food processor to coarse crumbs, the texture of wet sand.",
                   "Add the roasted peppers, tomato, garlic, vinegar, paprika, and salt and blend, streaming in the olive oil, until you have a thick, rusty, spoonable sauce. It should cling, not pour.",
                   "Trim the roots off the onions but leave them otherwise whole, greens and all. Get a grill screaming hot, or set your broiler to high with a rack close to the element.",
                   "Char the onions in a single layer until the outer skin is genuinely blackened on all sides, 8 to 12 minutes, turning once. Burnt is the technique, not a mistake.",
                   "Stack the charred onions on a sheet of newspaper or foil and wrap them tightly. Let them steam in their own heat for 15 minutes; this finishes cooking the sweet interior.",
                   "Show everyone the move: pinch the charred base, pull, and the blackened skin slides off leaving the silky white core. Dip deep into the romesco and lower it in from above.",
                   "Serve outdoors if you can, with plenty of napkins and the romesco bowl in the middle. Blackened fingers are part of the meal.",
               ],
               note: "The wrap-and-steam rest does half the cooking; unwrap early and the centers stay squeaky. And char harder than feels reasonable, since the burnt layer is discarded.",
               shortcut: "Good jarred romesco exists at many supermarkets and saves 20 minutes. Most are smoother and sweeter than homemade, so sharpen with a splash of vinegar."),
        Recipe(dishID: "spain-gilda", serves: 4, prepMinutes: 10, cookMinutes: 0, difficulty: 1,
               ingredients: [
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "12", item: "oil-packed anchovy fillets, the best jar in the store"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "24", item: "pickled guindilla peppers (or mild pepperoncini)"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "12", item: "pitted manzanilla olives"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "2 tbsp", item: "olive oil"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 splash", item: "brine from the pepper jar"),
               ],
               steps: [
                   "Drain the anchovies and lay them on a paper towel. Handle them like the precious things they are; a torn anchovy still tastes fine but loses the drape.",
                   "Trim the stems from the guindillas. If using pepperoncini, slice them in half lengthwise so their vinegar bite spreads through the bite instead of arriving all at once.",
                   "Build each skewer on a toothpick in the classic order: one pepper, an anchovy folded in an S-curve around an olive, then a second pepper to close.",
                   "Line the gildas up on a plate, drizzle with the olive oil, and add a small splash of pepper brine over the top. They should glisten.",
                   "Let them sit 10 minutes so the brine, oil, and anchovy salt start trading places, then serve standing up, before dinner, with something cold to drink. One bite per skewer, no nibbling.",
               ],
               note: "This is shopping, not cooking, so the anchovy decides everything. Cheap tinned fillets taste of salt and regret; spend up on Cantabrian-style anchovies if you can.",
               shortcut: "Green pimento-stuffed olives from any supermarket work for manzanillas. Skip anything labeled black or ripe olive, which turns the bite soft and flat."),
        Recipe(dishID: "spain-churros", serves: 4, prepMinutes: 15, cookMinutes: 30, difficulty: 2,
               ingredients: [
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 cup", item: "all-purpose flour"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 cup", item: "water"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1/2 tsp", item: "salt"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 tsp", item: "sugar, plus more for dusting"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "4 cups", item: "neutral frying oil"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "6 oz", item: "dark chocolate, chopped"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "1 1/2 cups", item: "whole milk"),
                   RecipeIngredient(amount: "2 tsp", item: "cornstarch"),
               ],
               steps: [
                   "Boil the water with the salt and 1 tsp sugar, pull it off the heat, and dump in the flour all at once. Stir hard with a wooden spoon until it forms a smooth, stiff ball.",
                   "Let the dough cool 10 minutes, then load it into a sturdy piping bag fitted with a large closed star tip. The star ridges are structural; a plain tube can burst in the oil.",
                   "Make the chocolate: whisk the cornstarch into the cold milk, heat until steaming, then add the chocolate and whisk over low heat until thick enough to coat a spoon heavily.",
                   "Heat the oil in a deep, wide pot to 350°F. Test with a scrap of dough; it should sizzle at once and brown in about 2 minutes.",
                   "Pipe 6-inch lengths directly into the oil, cutting each free with scissors, three or four at a time. Stand back as you pipe; the dough can spit.",
                   "Fry, turning once, until deep golden and rigid, 4 to 5 minutes; pale churros collapse as they cool. Drain on a rack and dust lightly with sugar.",
                   "Pour the thick chocolate into small cups and serve immediately, churros standing in a glass, the way a churreria does at dawn. Dip deep; the chocolate is a sauce, not a drink.",
               ],
               note: "The dough must be stiff and the oil truly at temperature. Wet dough or cool oil gives greasy, hollow-less churros, and dough piped from too high splashes.",
               shortcut: "A cookie press or a zip-top bag with a star tip replaces the piping bag. Squeeze carefully; thin bags can split under this stiff dough."),
    ]

    static let all: [Recipe] = recipes_italy + recipes_france + recipes_spain
}
